# DocGen — EI Ticket 3: Material identity on a later page with no element data (Propyl Gallate)

**Confirmed from the actual source document** (`WI-GLOB-QA-0529 EI declaration.PDF`). This is a third, distinct failure mode in the same family as the Fujicalin scope-split (T3) and the Aerosil scope-narrowing bug — but neither of those fixes covers this case. Do not attempt to patch this with the existing `_ei_scope_same` merge logic; it is the wrong mechanism (explained below).

## The confirmed document structure

- **Pages 1-3**: generic Dr. Reddy's WI-GLOB-QA-0529 template — a 30-element checklist (Cd, Pb, As, Hg, Pd, Pt, ... B), almost entirely "No/No". Only Pb has any data: "2ppm", frequency "6 months for third party test", "Yes — per customer request". No material name appears anywhere on these three pages.
- **Page 4 (the LAST page)**: contains the actual identity — **"Name of the material: Propyl gallate"**, "Manufacturer Name: Wufeng Chicheng Biotech Co., LTD", address, signature, company seal. This page has NO element rows at all — it is purely an identity/signature page.

## Why this produces "no annexure match"

1. `META_PROMPT` (the metadata-extraction call) runs only on `page_images[0]`. Page 1 is the generic checklist header with no material name — so the annexure's top-level `material_name` is extracted as something generic or blank, never "Propyl gallate."
2. The per-page prompt (`make_ei_prompt`) does run on page 4 and, per Section G, could tag `material_name: "Propyl gallate"` — but Section G's tagging attaches names to ELEMENT ROWS, and page 4 has zero element rows to tag. There is nothing for that mechanism to attach the name to.
3. Result: the Pb data (page 1) ends up associated with a generic/blank material identity, and "Propyl gallate" (page 4) never gets linked to any data. When the ingredient "Propyl gallate" is matched against annexures, no annexure resolves to that identity — hence "no annexure match," even though the document was fully uploaded and processed.

## Why this is NOT the same fix as T3 (Fujicalin) or the Aerosil ticket

- Fujicalin's bug: ONE material, its name spelled two ways across pages, both spellings correctly attached to their own element rows — fixed by merging equivalent scope-keys.
- Aerosil's bug: SIX genuinely different materials in one cluster, correct per-row scoping, but the wrong one being selected — fixed by normalizing the scope-selection comparison.
- **This bug**: the identity statement and the element data are on entirely different pages, and the page with the identity has no data of its own to tag. Neither a scope-merge nor a scope-selection fix applies — there is no scope conflict here, there is a missing LINK between an identity found on one page and data found on another page in the same document.

Do not reuse `_ei_scope_same` or the Aerosil scope-selection normalization for this. This needs a fallback at the material-name resolution level, not the scope-comparison level.

## The fix

When the top-level, page-1-derived `material_name` is missing, generic, or matches the document's own boilerplate title (e.g. "Manufacturer Declaration on Elemental Impurities Information" — the template's own heading, not a substance name), fall back to any material name surfaced by the per-page extraction anywhere in the document — including a page that has no element rows of its own. That name then becomes the annexure's material identity for matching purposes, regardless of which page it was found on or whether that page carried any data.

Concretely: after the per-page loop completes, if `meta.get("material_name")` is empty or equals a known boilerplate/template title, scan all per-page results for any non-empty `material_name` value (from Section G's tagging or an equivalent per-page identity field) and use the first one found as the document-level `material_name`. Do not require that page to also contain elements — a document's identity statement and its data table may legitimately be on different pages.

## Why this cannot break other documents

- This is a FALLBACK, triggered only when the page-1 metadata extraction already failed to produce a real material name. Any document where page 1 correctly names the material (the normal case, unaffected) never reaches this fallback path.
- It does not change how element data itself is read, scoped, or calculated — only how a document's overall material identity is resolved when the standard page-1 path comes up empty.
- It does not touch `_ei_scope_same`, the Fujicalin merge logic, or the Aerosil scope-selection normalization — this is an independent code path.

